{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And the remainder of the flesh of the sacrifice on the third day</b> — the meaning of the verse is not that what remains until the third day is burned, while it may be eaten at night, for our Rabbis already learned (Zevaḥim 56a) from the verse in the parashah of \"You shall be holy\" (Leviticus 19:6): \"and what remains until the third day shall be burned with fire,\" that whatever remains after two days altogether requires burning. Rather, \"on the third day\" is not joined with \"and the remainder,\" but is joined with \"shall be burned with fire\" — it says that the remainder of the flesh of the sacrifice that was not eaten on the day of its offering and on the following day shall be burned with fire on the third day, in the morning. This is what they said (Zevaḥim 55a): they are eaten for two days and one night, for on the second night it is neither eaten nor burned, since Scripture required that the burning of these disqualified offerings be by day, just as the offering of valid ones is by day."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:33.024923197Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}